# Attack Shark X11 2.4 GHz receiver (VID 1d57 / PID fa60).
#
# The receiver is a four-interface composite HID device. USB interface #2 is
# the vendor configuration channel that pushes the battery and DPI stage
# reports, and it is the only channel the application opens.
#
# Why this is split into two rules
# --------------------------------
# Every ATTRS match in a single rule must resolve to the same parent device,
# and udev silently refuses to match otherwise. A first attempt at this file
# combined ATTRS{idVendor}/ATTRS{idProduct} (which live on the USB *device*)
# with ATTRS{bInterfaceNumber} (which lives on the USB *interface*), and the
# rule matched nothing at all: no ACL, no symlink, and no error anywhere.
# The rules below are split so that each one only ever asks attributes of a
# single parent, following the same pattern as the system's own
# 60-steam-input.rules.

# Rule 1: access for the logged-in user.
# Both attributes belong to the USB device, so they agree on their parent.
#
# Belt and braces, because on Linux Mint 22.3 / Cinnamon the ACL route did not
# actually work for this device:
#
#   * GROUP + MODE is what grants access in practice. plugdev is the Debian and
#     Ubuntu convention for hotpluggable devices, and the desktop user is
#     already a member. On another distribution, either add the user to
#     plugdev or change GROUP to a group they are in.
#   * TAG+="uaccess" is kept for systems where logind does grant the ACL. It is
#     harmless when it does not: it produces no error, just no ACL.
#
# Measured on this machine: the uaccess tag is applied to the node and to all
# four of the receiver's hidraw nodes, but no ACL appears, because no device in
# this receiver's parent chain ever gets ID_SEAT, so logind has no seat to
# grant it for. /dev/uinput and /dev/snd/controlC0 do get a user ACL, which is
# how the difference was pinned down.
#
# This unavoidably also covers the receiver's keyboard, mouse and
# consumer-control channels, because they share the same USB device. That is
# the pattern every game-controller rule on this system uses, and those
# channels are already reachable by the user through /dev/input.
SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", KERNEL=="hidraw*", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1d57", ATTRS{idProduct}=="fa60", MODE="0660", GROUP="plugdev", TAG+="uaccess"

# Rule 2: a stable path to the configuration channel.
# The USB interface's modalias carries vendor, product *and* interface number
# in one attribute, so unlike rule 1 this needs no cross-parent matching at all.
# Written as "usb:v1D57pFA60*in02" so a firmware revision or a change to the
# interface's class fields does not break it.
#
# The hidraw node number is not stable: this receiver has been seen as hidraw2
# and as hidraw3 on different machines, so the application prefers this symlink
# and falls back to scanning sysfs if it is missing.
SUBSYSTEM=="hidraw", KERNEL=="hidraw*", ATTRS{modalias}=="usb:v1D57pFA60*in02", SYMLINK+="attackshark-x11"
